                        "webSearchUrl": "https://www.bing.com/entityexplore?q=Pansy&filters=sid:%22d4ec8d1d-37d4-abdf-8941-b88346e4236e%22&elv=AXXfrEiqqD9r3GuelwApulpKCfKXvP1aQ5OLC4!k1PPJf7!XPRBkgsQeV0u3oDkHdlqv84!kjmNXkNr2dR0akzf8yJvDPx3O8I0vf8M!gqkX",
                        "name": "Pansy",
                        "image": {
                            "name": "Pansy",
                            "thumbnailUrl": "http://www.bing.com/th?id=OSK._rpWRtrrjV_YJhExKEfV59CmDS-aNmJESqzzqETPsxQ&w=110&h=110&c=7",
                            "provider": [
                                {
                                    "_type": "Organization",
                                    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pansy"
                                }
                            ],
                            "hostPageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b8/PansyScan_%28cropped%29.jpg",
                            "width": 110,
                            "height": 110,
                            "sourceWidth": 1685,
                            "sourceHeight": 2184
                        },
                        "description": "The garden pansy is a type of polychromatic large-flowered hybrid plant cultivated as a garden flower. It is derived by hybridization from several species in the section Melanium of the genus Viola, particularly V. tricolor, a wildflower of Europe and western Asia known as heartsease. It is sometimes known as V. tricolor var. hortensis, but this scientific name is suspect. While V. tricolor var. hortensis Groenland & R\u00fcmpler is a synonym of Viola \u00d7 wittrockiana, V. tricolor var. hortensis DC. refers to a horticultural variety of wild pansy that had been illustrated in Flora Danica in 1777 before the existence of Viola \u00d7 wittrockiana.",
