                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.6",
                        "name": "Sorghastrum nutans (L.) Nash - Department of Ecology, Evolution, and ...",
                        "url": "https://www.eeob.iastate.edu/research/IowaGrasses/speciespages/SorghNutan/SorghNutan.html",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://www.eeob.iastate.edu/research/IowaGrasses/speciespages/SorghNutan/SorghNutan.html",
                        "snippet": "Sorghastrum nutans. (L.) Nash. Indian grass was one of the principal grasses of the tall grass prairie that dominated the central United States prior to European settlement and in Iowa is still common along roadsides and in prairie remnants. The native range of Indian grass extends from Canada to Mexico.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-10-31T02:14:00.0000000Z",
                        "cachedPageUrl": "http://cc.bingj.com/cache.aspx?q=Indian+Grass+(biology+OR+information+OR+botany+OR+scientific)+-blog+-shopping&d=4922260289553005&mkt=en-US&setlang=en-US&w=KH58ENzBuHu-YtMycZsJRBiKjqvcqyvt",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": false
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.7",
                        "name": "Native Grass Identification - USDA",
                        "url": "https://efotg.sc.egov.usda.gov/references/Public/SC/Native_Grass_Identification.pdf",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://efotg.sc.egov.usda.gov/references/Public/SC/Native_Grass_Identification.pdf",
                        "snippet": "Indiangrass is 4-7\u2019 tall warm season bunch grass with a graceful, fountain-like habit. A distinguishing trait for this grass is the \"rifle- sight\" where the leaf blade connects with the stem. The leaf blade becomes very narrow where it attaches to the stem. Mature seed heads open and become a light rust color and similar",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-02T02:03:00.0000000Z",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": true,
                        "siteName": "USDA"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.8",
                        "name": "Plant Guide - Natural Resources Conservation Service",
                        "url": "https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/plantmaterials/etpmcpg13196.pdf",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/plantmaterials/etpmcpg13196.pdf",
                        "snippet": "Grazing: Indiangrass is sensitive to overgrazing and will decrease under excessive grazing pressure (Weaver 1968). Begin grazing when the grass is 12 to 18 inches tall (Henning 1993). Do not graze the foliage lower than 8 inches. Use rotational grazing and proper livestock stocking rates to maintain stand productivity.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-03T02:23:00.0000000Z",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": true,
                        "siteName": "Natural Resources Conservation Service"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.9",
                        "name": "Kansas Wildflowers and Grasses - Indian grass",
                        "url": "https://kswildflower.org/grass_details.php?grassID=36",
                        "datePublished": "2021-03-07T00:00:00.0000000",
                        "datePublishedDisplayText": "Mar 7, 2021",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://kswildflower.org/grass_details.php?grassID=36",
                        "snippet": "Indian grass is very nutritious and is readily grazed by livestock, but it is only moderately palatable when mature. It decreases when grazed heavily. Birds and small mammals consume the seeds. Comments: Indian grass is one of our most important native tall grasses.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-02T08:51:00.0000000Z",
                        "cachedPageUrl": "http://cc.bingj.com/cache.aspx?q=Indian+Grass+(biology+OR+information+OR+botany+OR+scientific)+-blog+-shopping&d=4607460661133430&mkt=en-US&setlang=en-US&w=n_5TtDm4wv4vOxtmRkIS3Sj9Ue7pV17f",
