                        "siteName": "Plants of Texas Rangelands"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.12",
                        "name": "Indian Ricegrass - USU",
                        "url": "https://extension.usu.edu/rangeplants/grasses-and-grasslikes/indian-ricegrass",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://extension.usu.edu/rangeplants/grasses-and-grasslikes/indian-ricegrass",
                        "snippet": "Ecological Adaptions: Indian ricegrass is one of the more widely adapted native grasses of Utah rangelands. It is drought tolerant and found on most of Utah's range sites. It grows at elevations between 3,000 and 10,000 feet. Soils: Adapted to a wide variety of soils, but does not do well on wet or poorly drained soils.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-03T12:18:00.0000000Z",
                        "cachedPageUrl": "http://cc.bingj.com/cache.aspx?q=Indian+Grass+(biology+OR+information+OR+botany+OR+scientific)+-blog+-shopping&d=4512400146366995&mkt=en-US&setlang=en-US&w=kgZZgWbMf_EEAOhKyCHde9vgmm85YfHo",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": false,
                        "siteName": "Utah State University Extension"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.13",
                        "name": "Indian Ricegrass - US Forest Service",
                        "url": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/wildflowers/plant-of-the-week/achnatherum_hymenoides.shtml",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/wildflowers/plant-of-the-week/achnatherum_hymenoides.shtml",
                        "snippet": "Indian Ricegrass (. Achnatherum hymenoides. (Roemer & J.A. Schultes) Barkworth) By Forest Jay Gauna. As the common name implies, Native Americans utilized this species of grass in a manner analogous to that of rice by peoples of the Old World and their descendants. The name in Spanish is essentially the same: \u201c arroz indio.\u201d.",
