                        "webSearchUrl": "https://www.bing.com/entityexplore?q=Sweet+potato&filters=sid:%2206bd337a-8382-a371-74df-37b49dfc8e4e%22&elv=AXXfrEiqqD9r3GuelwApulpKCfKXvP1aQ5OLC4!k1PPJf7!XPRBkgsQeV0u3oDkHdlqv84!kjmNXkNr2dR0akzf8yJvDPx3O8I0vf8M!gqkX",
                        "name": "Sweet potato",
                        "image": {
                            "name": "Sweet potato",
                            "thumbnailUrl": "http://www.bing.com/th?id=OSK.e494645bdf8ede66048f04507f56075c&w=110&h=110&c=7",
                            "provider": [
                                {
                                    "_type": "Organization",
                                    "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweet_potato"
                                }
                            ],
                            "hostPageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/Ipomoea_batatas_006.JPG",
                            "width": 110,
                            "height": 110,
                            "sourceWidth": 2146,
                            "sourceHeight": 1611
                        },
                        "description": "The sweet potato is a dicotyledonous plant that belongs to the bindweed or morning glory family, Convolvulaceae. Its large, starchy, sweet-tasting tuberous roots are used as a root vegetable. The young shoots and leaves are sometimes eaten as greens. Cultivars of the sweet potato have been bred to bear tubers with flesh and skin of various colors. Sweet potato is only distantly related to the common potato, both being in the order Solanales. Although darker sweet potatoes are often referred to as \"yams\" in parts of North America, the species is even more distant from the true yams, which are monocots in the order Dioscoreales.",
