                        "name": "Liatris - Wikipedia",
                        "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liatris",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liatris",
                        "snippet": "Liatris ligulistylis with goldenrod soldier beetles (Chauliognathus pennsylvanicus) on it. Liatris (/ l a\u026a \u02c8 \u00e6 t r \u026a s / [2]), commonly known as gayfeather [3] and blazing star [4] [5] is a genus of flowering plants in the tribe Eupatorieae within the family Asteraceae native to North America (Canada, United States, Mexico and the Bahamas). [6] [7] [4] Some species are used as ornamental ...",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-03T10:03:00.0000000Z",
                        "cachedPageUrl": "http://cc.bingj.com/cache.aspx?q=Prairie+Gayfeather+(biology+OR+information+OR+botany+OR+scientific)+-blog+-shopping&d=4684409294225963&mkt=en-US&setlang=en-US&w=cPiofFrXO_lSOU9mXh_SrSYNkDLRW7LR",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": false,
                        "siteName": "Wikipedia"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.5",
                        "name": "Natural Resources Conservation Service Plant Guide - USDA",
                        "url": "https://plants.sc.egov.usda.gov/DocumentLibrary/plantguide/pdf/pg_lipy.pdf",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://plants.sc.egov.usda.gov/DocumentLibrary/plantguide/pdf/pg_lipy.pdf",
                        "snippet": "studied. Although more prevalent in prairie areas, this forb is found in Texas and Louisiana on the western side of the pine belt in longleaf pine and bluestem areas (Grelen and Hughes, 1984; Grelen and Duvall, 1966). Art (1991) reported that gayfeather grew best on moist, well drained, and slightly acidic to neutral (pH 5.5 to 7.0) soils.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-10-30T14:14:00.0000000Z",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": true,
                        "siteName": "USDA Plants Database"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.6",
                        "name": "Plant Guide - Natural Resources Conservation Service",
                        "url": "https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/plantmaterials/kspmcpg7800.pdf",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://www.nrcs.usda.gov/plantmaterials/kspmcpg7800.pdf",
                        "snippet": ": Dotted gayfeather belongs to the composite or sunflower family (Asteraceae). Dotted gayfeather is a native long lived herbaceous forb that is 20 to 80 cm tall, with stems single or in clusters from a woody rootstock. This species taproot system is remarkably extensive; its lateral branches spread 1.0 to 1.5 meters.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-01T00:35:00.0000000Z",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": true,
                        "siteName": "Natural Resources Conservation Service"
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "https://api.bing.microsoft.com/api/v7/#WebPages.7",
                        "name": "Plant Fact Sheet - USDA Plants Database",
                        "url": "https://plants.usda.gov/DocumentLibrary/factsheet/pdf/fs_lipu.pdf",
                        "isFamilyFriendly": true,
                        "displayUrl": "https://plants.usda.gov/DocumentLibrary/factsheet/pdf/fs_lipu.pdf",
                        "snippet": "Description and Adaptation. General: Dotted gayfeather belongs to the composite or sunflower family (Asteraceae). Dotted gayfeather is a native, perennial species that is 1 to 2.5 feet tall with one to many stems arising from a woody crown. It has a deep tap root which extends up to 7 feet in heavy clay soils and to 16 feet in lighter soils.",
                        "dateLastCrawled": "2024-11-04T15:27:00.0000000Z",
                        "language": "en",
                        "isNavigational": false,
                        "noCache": true,
                        "siteName": "USDA Plants Database"
