                        "displayUrl": "https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/acer/saccharum",
                        "snippet": "Sugar maple is an emblematic and common tree of the New England landscape, widely planted along roadsides and sugar bushes in order to harvest its maple syrup. Trees planted by colonial settlers survive to this day, often with large, gnarly trunks and deeply fissured bark. The silhouette of the sugar maple leaf is the centerpiece of the ...",
                        "deepLinks": [
                            {
                                "name": "Acer Campestre",
                                "url": "https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/acer/campestre/",
                                "snippet": "Hedge maple is native to Europe, where it commonly inhabits hedgerows between farm fields; hence its common name. It tends to branch near the ground (and tolerates heavy pruning), giving it a shrubby look, like a scruffy Norway maple. Like Norway maple, it has milky sap.",
                                "deepLinks": []
                            },
                            {
                                "name": "Acer Platanoides",
                                "url": "https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/acer/platanoides/",
                                "snippet": "The leaves of Norway maple closely resemble those of sugar maple, but the petioles exude a white sap when broken. The gray bark has even grooves. First introduced by the famous Pennsylvania botanist, John Bartram, in 1756, this species is very popular as a hardy street tree.",
                                "deepLinks": []
                            }
